// A representation of procedural noise, generated by a noise source, that you can use to process, transform, or combine noise.
//
// Using procedural noise requires three steps: Select the subclass that generates the style of noise you’d like, and configure its properties to customize the noise generation algorithm. Create a object from that noise source. A noise source generates a field of floating-point noise values between and across an infinite, three-dimensional domain—a noise object represents this field. Using methods you can process the values in the noise field or combine values from multiple noise fields. Create a object from the noise object. A noise map samples values from a finite, two-dimensional slice of the noise field to create a concrete output. You can then read those values directly, use the class to generate texture images, or use the class to generate tile maps. objects are lightweight. Because each noise object represents only a specific configuration of noise generation and processing steps, there’s little computation cost to creating noise objects from noise sources and applying operations to process, transform, or combine them. After you create noise objects and apply operations, creating a object from the resulting noise object performs only the computation needed to generate final output.


